  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1221 AM CDT Thu Apr 23 2026\n\nNo significant changes from the last few forecasts for the long \nterm. We'll start with zonal flow aloft on Friday that will \ngradually transition to southwest flow through the weekend, as \nupper level troughing develops over the western CONUS. From Sunday\ninto Monday, a shortwave trough will eject out into the central \nand southern plains from the Desert Southwest. While this feature \ncould normally provide a chance for showers and thunderstorms for \nour area, most of the upper level energy will be too far north for\nour area to see much more than a brief low (less than 20%) chance\nfor showers and thunderstorms Sunday night. Other than that, \nmodels continue to indicate the possibility of a weak cold front \nmoving into the area around the middle of next week that may also \nprovide a low (20%-30%) chance for showers and thunderstorms. \nOtherwise, temperatures will be above normal with highs ranging \nfrom the mid 80s to mid 90s, and lows mainly in the 60s most days.\nWe could see a modest cool down behind the front next week.",
